          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Brought up something that looked like old coffee grounds this morning after a night of retching.",
          "answer": "**Seek emergency medical care now.** Vomit that looks like coffee grounds is listed in the gastrointestinal bleeding guidance as a reason to call emergency services immediately (source: \"Gastrointestinal bleeding,\" gastroenterology and emergency medicine educational summary). This is true even if it was only a small amount and the vomiting has stopped. The automated risk check returned a \"routine\" tier because its fixed rule table did not match this wording. That result does not override the guidance, so please do not treat this as routine.\n\nCall emergency services, or have someone take you to an emergency department, without waiting to see whether it happens again. Do not drive yourself. Call immediately if you have any of these, which the same guidance lists as signs of significant blood loss:\n- dizziness, faintness, or collapse\n- a racing or pounding heartbeat\n- cold, clammy skin\n- confusion\n- breathlessness\n- black, tarry, foul-smelling stools\n\nTell the clinicians if you take a blood thinner (anticoagulant) or have known liver disease or enlarged veins in the food pipe (varices). The guidance treats bleeding in these situations as an emergency.\n\nI could not retrieve material on the possible causes, such as tears from forceful retching, so I cannot describe them from my sources. A clinician will need to assess the cause. Your sources did not answer that question.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
